and uses the already-moved rook on h1 which is eligible for castling (RWF0 on h1 and KWF0 both indicate no prior move).\n- **Q: d1-d2 / d1-e2**: Queen moves that defend e2 or prepare castling, but slightly premature without king safety.\n- **N: f3-e5**: Jumping to e5 attacks f7 and pressures Black’s kingside, but may be met with ...f6 or ...Nxe5, and it risks losing time if challenged.\n\nThe strongest move is **K: e1-g1**, as it castles kingside — securing the king, activating the rook on h1, and taking advantage of all conditions being met (no prior move, no pieces between, king not in check, and path is safe — f1 and g1 are unattacked).\n\nReason:  \nCastling is urgent for king safety and improves piece coordination. All conditions are satisfied: the king and h1 rook haven’t moved, there are no pieces between, and the king does not pass through or end up in check. Black currently has no immediate threats, but delaying castling risks future tactics. Other developing moves are good, but none match the strategic priority of safeguarding the king.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-g1'}
