P: f2-f4** opens lines but weakens the diagonal to g1.\n\nBut **B: c1-f4** is strong: it develops actively, threatens to trade off Black’s well-placed knight on e5, and keeps the king safe.\n\nBlack's knight on e5 is centrally placed and strong — trading it may be good, but only if we don’t waste time. Bf4 doesn’t force a trade, but it pressures it.\n\nMoreover, after Bf4, if Black plays ...f6, we can play Bxe5, opening the e-file or disrupting.\n\nAnother option: **Qd1-d3**, connecting with possible battery on the h8-a1 diagonal via Q-B.\n\nBut **Bf4** improves a piece, increases pressure, and is harmonious.\n\nReason:  \nB: c1-f4 develops the bishop to an active square, exerts immediate pressure on the center (e5 knight), avoids blocking the queen, and prepares for harmonious development. It does not weaken the king’s position and maintains flexibility in pawn structure.\n\nAnswer: B: c1-f4"}
